// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 3.0 Security Architecture
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 3.1 Architecture models (30) ----------
{o:"3.1",q:"Under the cloud shared responsibility model for IaaS, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",c:["The customer","The cloud provider","Both equally","The hypervisor vendor"],a:0,e:["Correct. In IaaS the provider secures the physical hardware and hypervisor. The customer owns the OS, apps, data and configuration.","In IaaS the provider stops at the virtualization layer.","Responsibility is divided by layer, not shared equally.","The hypervisor vendor doesn't manage your guest OS."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company uses a SaaS CRM. Which responsibility stays with the CUSTOMER?",c:["Managing user accounts, access and the data entered","Patching the CRM's servers","Physical data center security","Hypervisor updates"],a:0,e:["Correct. Even in SaaS, the customer owns its data, identities and access configuration.","The SaaS provider patches its platform.","The provider handles physical security.","The provider handles infrastructure."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company combines an on-prem data center with Azure and needs workloads to talk securely between them. Which consideration is MOST essential for seamless operation?",c:["Network connectivity and integration between environments","Moving all staff to remote work","Using only SaaS applications","Eliminating the on-prem firewall"],a:0,e:["Correct. Hybrid cloud depends on reliable, secure connectivity (VPN, dedicated links) and integration of identity and management.","Remote work is unrelated.","That's a different model.","You still need perimeter controls."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Developers upload functions to the cloud and are billed per execution; they never manage servers. Which model is this?",c:["Serverless (FaaS)","IaaS","On-premises virtualization","Microservices on bare metal"],a:0,e:["Correct. Serverless/FaaS abstracts all servers. The provider runs and scales the code on demand.","In IaaS you manage the VMs.","On-prem virtualization means you run the hypervisor.","Bare metal means you run the hardware."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A team defines all VPCs, subnets and firewall rules in Terraform files stored in Git. Which is a key SECURITY benefit?",c:["Consistent, repeatable, reviewable configurations with less drift","It eliminates the need for access control","It makes systems air-gapped","It removes the need for patching"],a:0,e:["Correct. IaC makes infrastructure version-controlled and reviewable, and every deployment is identical, which reduces misconfiguration.","IAM is still required.","IaC has nothing to do with network isolation.","Images still need patching."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"What is a security RISK specific to Infrastructure as Code?",c:["A single error or embedded secret in a template is replicated across every deployment","It always requires physical servers","It can't be version-controlled","It prevents automation"],a:0,e:["Correct. A mistake or hard-coded secret spreads everywhere the template is used. Scan templates and keep secrets out of code.","IaC is typically used in the cloud.","IaC is designed for version control.","IaC enables automation."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An app is broken into 40 small services that communicate over APIs. What security implication is MOST significant?",c:["Larger attack surface and more complex service-to-service authentication and authorization","Fewer endpoints to monitor","A single point of failure","No need for API security"],a:0,e:["Correct. Microservices multiply endpoints and interactions, so each call needs authentication, authorization and monitoring.","There are more endpoints, not fewer.","Microservices reduce single points of failure.","APIs become the main attack surface."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which technology separates the network control plane from the data plane so traffic can be managed centrally through software?",c:["Software-defined networking (SDN)","VLANs","Air gap","Containerization"],a:0,e:["Correct. SDN centralizes control-plane logic in a controller that programs the data-plane devices.","VLANs segment layer 2 networks but don't separate the planes.","An air gap is physical isolation.","Containers package applications."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which technology runs multiple isolated applications that share the host OS kernel?",c:["Containerization","Virtualization with a type 1 hypervisor","Air-gapped systems","Mainframes"],a:0,e:["Correct. Containers share the host kernel and isolate at the process level. They're lighter than VMs, but a kernel flaw affects them all.","VMs each have their own OS kernel.","An air gap is about network isolation.","A mainframe is a platform type."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"What is a key difference between a VM and a container?",c:["Each VM runs a full guest OS; containers share the host OS kernel","Containers always need more resources than VMs","VMs can't be isolated","Containers require a type 2 hypervisor"],a:0,e:["Correct. VMs virtualize hardware and run separate OSes. Containers virtualize the OS and share its kernel.","Containers are generally lighter.","VMs are strongly isolated.","Containers use a container runtime, not a hypervisor."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A classified research network has no physical or wireless connection to any other network. Data moves only by approved, scanned media. What model is this?",c:["Air-gapped","Logical segmentation","Hybrid cloud","SDN"],a:0,e:["Correct. An air gap is complete physical separation from other networks. Removable media then becomes the main risk.","Logical segmentation still shares infrastructure.","Hybrid cloud is connected by design.","SDN is a networking approach."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company splits its network into VLANs for HR, Finance and Guest, all on the same physical switches. What is this?",c:["Logical segmentation","Physical isolation","Air gap","Serverless"],a:0,e:["Correct. VLANs separate traffic logically on shared hardware.","Physical isolation uses separate hardware.","An air gap means no connection at all.","Serverless is a compute model."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A factory uses systems that control valves, motors and sensors, supervised from a central HMI. Which term BEST describes this environment?",c:["Industrial control system (ICS) / SCADA","Embedded system only","Serverless","Microservices"],a:0,e:["Correct. ICS/SCADA monitors and controls physical industrial processes.","Embedded systems are components; the whole supervisory environment is ICS/SCADA.","Serverless is a cloud model.","Microservices is a software architecture."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"What is the MOST common security challenge with ICS/SCADA systems?",c:["Long lifecycles, limited patching, and legacy protocols without authentication or encryption","They patch automatically every day","They only run in the cloud","They have no network connectivity ever"],a:0,e:["Correct. ICS runs for decades, uptime comes first, and protocols such as Modbus lack security, so segmentation and monitoring are critical.","Patching is rare and difficult.","They're usually on-prem.","Many are networked, which is the problem."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A smart thermostat runs a tiny OS on a microcontroller with limited memory and no easy way to update. Which term applies?",c:["Embedded system / IoT","Mainframe","Serverless","Hypervisor"],a:0,e:["Correct. Embedded and IoT devices are purpose-built with constrained resources and often weak update mechanisms.","A mainframe is large-scale computing.","Serverless is a cloud model.","A hypervisor runs VMs."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An automotive braking controller must respond within strict timing deadlines. What type of OS does it likely use?",c:["Real-time operating system (RTOS)","General-purpose desktop OS","Serverless runtime","Container OS"],a:0,e:["Correct. An RTOS guarantees deterministic, time-bounded responses for embedded and critical systems.","A desktop OS doesn't guarantee timing.","Serverless isn't used for this.","Containers are general-purpose."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which consideration refers to how quickly a system can be restored and brought back into service after failure?",c:["Ease of recovery","Scalability","Responsiveness","Cost"],a:0,e:["Correct. Ease of recovery covers how simply and quickly an architecture can be restored.","Scalability is handling growth.","Responsiveness is timely feedback to requests.","Cost is budget."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An architecture review asks: 'Can this design handle 10x the users next year?' Which consideration is this?",c:["Scalability","Availability","Resilience","Patch availability"],a:0,e:["Correct. Scalability is the ability to grow (or shrink) with demand.","Availability is uptime now.","Resilience is withstanding faults.","Patch availability is about updates."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A payment API must keep processing even if one data center loses power. Which consideration is being prioritized?",c:["Resilience / high availability","Responsiveness","Ease of deployment","Power efficiency"],a:0,e:["Correct. Resilience and availability mean continuing to function through failures, through redundancy and failover.","Responsiveness is speed.","Ease of deployment is about releases.","Power efficiency isn't the goal here."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"When evaluating architecture models, what does the 'inability to patch' consideration MOST apply to?",c:["Embedded, ICS and legacy systems that can't easily be updated","All SaaS applications","Containers built from current images","Serverless functions"],a:0,e:["Correct. Embedded, ICS and legacy systems often can't be patched, which forces compensating controls.","SaaS providers patch for you.","You can rebuild containers from a patched image.","The provider patches the serverless runtime."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company chooses a cloud region in its own country to satisfy data residency laws. Which architecture consideration drove this?",c:["Compliance / data sovereignty","Scalability","Power","Responsiveness"],a:0,e:["Correct. Laws requiring data to stay in-country drive region selection.","Growth isn't the driver.","Power isn't the driver.","Latency could be a side benefit, but compliance is the driver."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"What is a 'type 1' hypervisor?",c:["A bare-metal hypervisor that runs directly on hardware","A hypervisor that runs as an app on a desktop OS","A container runtime","A cloud access broker"],a:0,e:["Correct. Type 1 (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM) runs directly on hardware. It's more efficient and has less attack surface.","That's type 2 (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation).","Containers don't use a hypervisor.","A CASB is a cloud security broker."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company moves a monolithic application to the cloud unchanged, on a VM. What is the security implication compared with re-architecting?",c:["It inherits the same vulnerabilities and needs the customer to manage OS-level security","It becomes serverless automatically","The cloud provider now patches the app","It becomes air-gapped"],a:0,e:["Correct. 'Lift and shift' moves existing weaknesses along with the app, and in IaaS the customer still handles the OS and app.","Nothing becomes serverless.","The app remains the customer's responsibility.","It's now connected to the cloud, not isolated."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which cloud responsibility BEST describes the 'responsibility matrix'?",c:["A document defining which security tasks belong to the provider vs the customer","A list of employee roles","A firewall ruleset","An incident timeline"],a:0,e:["Correct. The responsibility matrix spells out, per service model, who handles each control.","That's an org chart.","That's configuration.","That's IR documentation."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A startup deploys identical workloads across AWS and GCP. Which BEST explains the security benefit?",c:["Reduced vendor lock-in and resilience if one provider fails (platform diversity)","Simpler security policy management","Fewer skills needed","Lower complexity"],a:0,e:["Correct. Multi-cloud adds resilience and avoids depending on one provider. The trade-off is more complexity.","Policies get harder to manage across providers.","You need skills in both.","Complexity increases."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which characteristic describes 'centralized' vs 'decentralized' architecture from a security view?",c:["Centralized is easier to manage but a single point of failure; decentralized is more resilient but harder to secure consistently","Decentralized always costs less","Centralized can't be monitored","They have identical risk"],a:0,e:["Correct. Centralized designs simplify control but concentrate risk. Decentralized designs spread risk but complicate governance.","Cost varies.","Centralized designs are often easier to monitor.","Their risk profiles differ."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A hospital buys networked infusion pumps. Which is the BEST security approach given they're IoT devices with vendor-controlled firmware?",c:["Segment them, restrict their traffic, and monitor for anomalies","Install antivirus on each pump","Give them internet access for updates any time","Treat them like laptops"],a:0,e:["Correct. Constrained, vendor-controlled devices are protected with network controls and monitoring.","Antivirus usually can't be installed on them.","Unrestricted internet access increases exposure.","They can't be managed like laptops."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A developer asks why the company's Kubernetes containers must not run as root. What is the BEST reason?",c:["If a container is compromised, root inside it makes escaping to the shared host kernel easier","Root containers run slower","Kubernetes forbids root entirely","Root is required for networking"],a:0,e:["Correct. Containers share the host kernel, so root inside a container raises the impact of an escape. Least privilege applies to containers too.","Performance isn't the reason.","Kubernetes allows it; policy should restrict it.","Networking doesn't require root."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which describes 'high availability' across zones?",c:["Deploying redundant instances in multiple availability zones so one zone's failure doesn't cause downtime","Using one large server","Backing up once a month","Using a single region with one zone"],a:0,e:["Correct. Spreading across zones removes single points of failure.","One server is a single point of failure.","Monthly backups are for recovery, not availability.","A single zone is a single point of failure."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company uses a hosted private cloud dedicated only to it. Which deployment model is this?",c:["Private cloud","Public cloud","Community cloud","Hybrid cloud"],a:0,e:["Correct. A private cloud is dedicated to one organization, whether on-prem or hosted.","Public cloud is shared multi-tenant.","Community cloud is shared by organizations with common needs.","Hybrid combines two or more models."]},

// ---------- 3.2 Secure enterprise infrastructure (32) ----------
{o:"3.2",q:"A company places its public web and mail servers in a zone separated from both the internet and the internal LAN. What is this zone?",c:["Screened subnet (DMZ)","Intranet","Air gap","VLAN trunk"],a:0,e:["Correct. A screened subnet (DMZ) hosts internet-facing services so a compromise there doesn't directly expose the LAN.","The intranet is internal.","An air gap has no connection.","A trunk carries multiple VLANs."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A security appliance must BLOCK malicious traffic in real time, not just alert. How must it be deployed?",c:["Inline","Passive via a TAP","On a SPAN port","Out-of-band only"],a:0,e:["Correct. Only an inline device sits in the traffic path and can drop packets. This is how an IPS works.","A TAP copies traffic, so the device only sees it.","A SPAN (mirror) port also gives a copy.","Out-of-band can't stop traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An analyst wants a full copy of traffic for analysis without any risk of disrupting the network. Which option is BEST?",c:["A network TAP / monitor port","An inline IPS","A proxy server","A load balancer"],a:0,e:["Correct. TAPs and monitor (SPAN) ports give a copy of traffic passively. If the sensor fails, traffic keeps flowing.","Inline devices can disrupt traffic if they fail.","Proxies are inline intermediaries.","Load balancers distribute traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"If an inline security appliance fails, which setting keeps traffic flowing (prioritizing availability)?",c:["Fail-open","Fail-closed","Fail-secure","Implicit deny"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fail-open lets traffic pass when the device fails, preserving availability at a security cost.","Fail-closed blocks all traffic.","Fail-secure is another term for fail-closed.","Implicit deny is a rule concept."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A bank decides that if its IPS crashes, all traffic should stop rather than pass uninspected. What mode is this?",c:["Fail-closed","Fail-open","Active/active","Monitor mode"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fail-closed prioritizes security (confidentiality and integrity) over availability.","Fail-open prioritizes availability.","Active/active is about redundancy.","Monitor mode is passive."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which firewall can make decisions based on the application (e.g., allow Teams but block BitTorrent on the same port)?",c:["Layer 7 / next-generation firewall","Layer 4 stateful firewall","Packet filter","Router ACL"],a:0,e:["Correct. Layer 7/NGFW inspects application content, so it can identify apps regardless of port.","Layer 4 only knows IPs, ports and connection state.","Packet filters see headers only.","Router ACLs work at layers 3/4."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A web app needs protection against SQL injection and XSS in HTTP requests. Which device is MOST appropriate?",c:["Web application firewall (WAF)","Layer 4 firewall","NAC","VPN concentrator"],a:0,e:["Correct. A WAF inspects HTTP/S for web attacks.","A layer 4 firewall can't see the SQLi payload.","NAC controls device admission.","A VPN concentrator creates tunnels."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A small business wants firewall, IPS, antivirus, content filtering and VPN in one appliance. What is it?",c:["Unified threat management (UTM)","WAF","Jump server","Load balancer"],a:0,e:["Correct. A UTM combines many security functions in one box. It's convenient but a single point of failure.","A WAF only protects web apps.","A jump server is for admin access.","A load balancer distributes traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Administrators must reach servers in a secure zone only through one hardened, monitored host. What is that host?",c:["Jump server (bastion host)","Proxy server","Honeypot","DNS server"],a:0,e:["Correct. A jump server is the single controlled entry point for admin access, which makes it easy to audit and harden.","A proxy handles user requests to other servers.","A honeypot is a decoy.","DNS resolves names."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Employees' web requests go through a server that caches content, filters URLs and hides internal IPs. What is it?",c:["Forward proxy","Reverse proxy","Load balancer","Jump server"],a:0,e:["Correct. A forward proxy acts for internal clients going out.","A reverse proxy sits in front of servers for inbound requests.","A load balancer spreads load across servers.","A jump server is for admins."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A server sits in front of web servers, terminates TLS and forwards requests to them, hiding their addresses. What is it?",c:["Reverse proxy","Forward proxy","Jump server","NAC"],a:0,e:["Correct. A reverse proxy handles inbound requests for servers and often does TLS offload, caching and WAF functions.","A forward proxy serves outbound clients.","A jump server is admin access.","NAC is admission control."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which device distributes incoming traffic across several web servers to improve availability and performance?",c:["Load balancer","IDS","Router","TAP"],a:0,e:["Correct. Load balancers spread traffic and route around failed servers.","An IDS detects.","A router forwards between networks.","A TAP copies traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Before a laptop can join the corporate network, the switch checks its certificate and verifies it has current antivirus. What technology is this?",c:["Network access control (NAC) with 802.1X","Port mirroring","VPN split tunnel","WAF"],a:0,e:["Correct. NAC authenticates the device (802.1X) and checks posture before granting access, or places it in a quarantine VLAN.","Mirroring copies traffic.","Split tunneling is a VPN routing choice.","A WAF protects web apps."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"In 802.1X, which component is the switch or wireless access point?",c:["Authenticator","Supplicant","Authentication server","Certificate authority"],a:0,e:["Correct. The authenticator (switch or AP) passes EAP messages between the client and the RADIUS server and enforces the result.","The supplicant is the client device.","The authentication server is usually RADIUS.","The CA issues certificates."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company needs a framework that supports many authentication methods (certificates, tokens, passwords) for wired and wireless 802.1X. Which should it use?",c:["EAP","WPA3","IPSec","PAP"],a:0,e:["Correct. EAP is an extensible framework that carries many methods (EAP-TLS, PEAP, etc.) over 802.1X.","WPA3 is Wi-Fi security, not a flexible auth framework for wired networks.","IPSec is VPN encryption.","PAP is a cleartext password protocol."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A switch is configured so only the first learned MAC address can use each port, and any other device shuts the port down. What feature is this?",c:["Port security","802.1Q trunking","Spanning tree","Port mirroring"],a:0,e:["Correct. Port security restricts ports by MAC address and can shut down on a violation.","802.1Q tags VLANs.","STP prevents loops.","Mirroring copies traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which VPN type connects two office networks permanently over the internet?",c:["Site-to-site VPN","Remote access VPN","Clientless SSL VPN for one user","TLS on a website"],a:0,e:["Correct. Site-to-site VPNs link networks, usually with IPSec between gateways.","Remote access VPNs connect individual users.","Clientless VPN is per-user browser access.","A TLS website protects one application."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Remote users need encrypted access to internal apps using only a web browser, no client software. Which is BEST?",c:["TLS (SSL) VPN, clientless","IPSec site-to-site","Telnet","L2TP without IPSec"],a:0,e:["Correct. A clientless TLS VPN runs through the browser over HTTPS.","Site-to-site links networks, not browsers.","Telnet is insecure.","L2TP alone provides no encryption."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"IPSec protects the entire original packet, including its headers, and adds a new header for transit between gateways. Which mode?",c:["Tunnel mode","Transport mode","AH only","IKE phase 1"],a:0,e:["Correct. Tunnel mode encapsulates the whole packet. It's used for site-to-site VPNs.","Transport mode protects only the payload and is used host to host.","AH provides integrity, not the encapsulation described.","IKE negotiates keys."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which IPSec protocol provides confidentiality (encryption) as well as integrity?",c:["ESP","AH","IKE","GRE"],a:0,e:["Correct. Encapsulating Security Payload encrypts and authenticates.","AH provides integrity and authentication only, with no encryption.","IKE negotiates security associations and keys.","GRE is a tunneling protocol with no encryption."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A branch office wants to route traffic over multiple internet links, managed centrally, with policy-based path selection. What technology is this?",c:["SD-WAN","SASE only","VLAN","NAC"],a:0,e:["Correct. SD-WAN manages WAN links in software with centralized policy.","SASE combines SD-WAN with cloud security; the question describes only SD-WAN.","A VLAN is local segmentation.","NAC is device admission."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants cloud-delivered security (SWG, CASB, ZTNA, firewall) combined with SD-WAN for remote users everywhere. What is this?",c:["SASE","UTM","DMZ","Jump server"],a:0,e:["Correct. Secure Access Service Edge combines networking and security as a cloud service close to users.","A UTM is an on-prem appliance.","A DMZ is a network zone.","A jump server is for admin access."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Where should an IDS sensor be placed to see traffic that got past the perimeter firewall?",c:["Inside the firewall, on the internal network or DMZ segment","On a user's laptop only","Outside the ISP","In the CEO's office"],a:0,e:["Correct. A sensor behind the firewall sees traffic that actually entered, which reduces noise and catches real threats.","A host agent is a HIDS, not network placement.","You don't control the ISP's network.","That's not a network location."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which BEST describes 'attack surface' reduction in infrastructure design?",c:["Minimizing exposed services, ports, interfaces and entry points","Adding more public IPs","Opening all ports for testing","Allowing any-to-any rules"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fewer exposed entry points leave fewer opportunities for attackers.","More public IPs means more exposure.","Opening ports increases the surface.","Any-to-any rules are the opposite."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A firewall rule order is: 1) allow any to web server 443, 2) deny all. A new rule to block one malicious IP to the web server is added at position 3. What happens?",c:["The malicious IP is still allowed because rule 1 matches first","The malicious IP is blocked","All traffic is blocked","The firewall ignores rule order"],a:0,e:["Correct. Firewalls process rules top-down and stop at the first match. The block rule has to go above rule 1.","The block is never reached.","Rule 1 still allows everyone else.","Order is critical."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which device security attribute describes inspecting traffic by placing the device directly between two network segments?",c:["Active / inline","Passive / tap","Monitor mode","Out-of-band"],a:0,e:["Correct. An inline (active) device can inspect and act on traffic.","Passive devices only watch copies.","Monitor mode is passive.","Out-of-band isn't in the path."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company's Wi-Fi has 802.1X enterprise authentication with a RADIUS server. Which protocol MOST likely carries accounting and authentication between the AP and server?",c:["RADIUS","TACACS+","LDAP only","Kerberos"],a:0,e:["Correct. RADIUS is the standard AAA protocol for network access (Wi-Fi and VPN).","TACACS+ is typically for device administration (Cisco admin logins).","LDAP may be the directory behind RADIUS.","Kerberos is domain authentication."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Network engineers need AAA for logins to routers and switches, with each command authorized and logged separately. Which protocol is BEST?",c:["TACACS+","RADIUS","PAP","SNMPv1"],a:0,e:["Correct. TACACS+ separates authentication, authorization and accounting, supports per-command authorization and encrypts the whole payload.","RADIUS combines authentication and authorization and only encrypts the password.","PAP is cleartext.","SNMPv1 is insecure monitoring."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which infrastructure consideration refers to choosing where a security device sits so it sees the right traffic?",c:["Device placement","Fail mode","Latency","Licensing"],a:0,e:["Correct. Placement (perimeter, DMZ, core, host) determines what a device can inspect.","Fail mode is about behavior when the device fails.","Latency is a performance factor.","Licensing is cost."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which option provides SECURE remote command-line administration of Linux servers?",c:["SSH","Telnet","FTP","HTTP"],a:0,e:["Correct. SSH encrypts sessions, runs on port 22, and supports key-based authentication.","Telnet is cleartext.","FTP transfers files in cleartext.","HTTP is unencrypted web traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which is TRUE of a layer 4 firewall compared with a layer 7 firewall?",c:["Layer 4 filters by IP, port and connection state but can't tell applications apart on the same port","Layer 4 inspects application payloads","Layer 4 blocks SQL injection","Layer 4 is always more secure"],a:0,e:["Correct. Layer 4 sees transport information only; layer 7 understands applications.","Payload inspection is layer 7.","SQLi needs a WAF or layer 7.","Layer 4 has less visibility."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company uses a firewall appliance between its internal network and the internet, and a second one between the DMZ and the internal network. What does this design achieve?",c:["Defense in depth with a screened subnet between two firewalls","A single point of failure","No segmentation","An air gap"],a:0,e:["Correct. A dual-firewall DMZ means an attacker must breach two different barriers to reach the LAN.","Two firewalls reduce single points of failure.","It is segmentation.","The networks are still connected."]},

// ---------- 3.3 Protecting data (22) ----------
{o:"3.3",q:"Which data STATE describes a customer record currently loaded in RAM while an application processes it?",c:["Data in use","Data at rest","Data in transit","Data sovereignty"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data being actively processed in memory or CPU is in use.","At rest means stored on disk.","In transit means moving across a network.","Sovereignty is a legal concept."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which protection is designed specifically for data in use?",c:["Secure enclaves / homomorphic encryption / confidential computing","TLS","Full-disk encryption","Tape backups"],a:0,e:["Correct. Enclaves and homomorphic techniques protect data while it is being processed.","TLS protects data in transit.","FDE protects data at rest.","Backups are for recovery."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A report shows customer SSNs as ***-**-6789 to call-center staff. What technique is this?",c:["Data masking","Tokenization","Hashing","Encryption"],a:0,e:["Correct. Masking hides part of the data while keeping its format and some usefulness.","Tokenization replaces the whole value with a token.","Hashing produces a fixed-length digest.","Encrypted text wouldn't be partially readable."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A payment processor replaces card numbers with random surrogate values. The real numbers are kept only in a secured vault that maps tokens back. What technique is this?",c:["Tokenization","Masking","Hashing","Steganography"],a:0,e:["Correct. Tokenization swaps real values for tokens with no mathematical relationship to them, and a secure vault holds the mapping. It reduces PCI scope.","Masking hides characters but doesn't use a vault.","Hashing isn't reversible through a vault.","Steganography hides data inside other data."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which method converts data into a fixed-length value that can't be reversed?",c:["Hashing","Tokenization","Encryption","Masking"],a:0,e:["Correct. A hash is a one-way, fixed-length function.","A token can be looked up in the vault.","Encryption can be reversed with the key.","Masking may keep part of the original data."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company's regulatory filings with personal details redacted are published on its website. Which classification do the published versions MOST likely have?",c:["Public","Confidential","Restricted","Critical"],a:0,e:["Correct. Once sensitive parts are redacted and the document is intentionally released, it's public.","Confidential data isn't meant for public release.","Restricted is the highest internal sensitivity.","Critical is about operational importance."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which type of data is governed by laws such as HIPAA, GDPR or PCI DSS?",c:["Regulated data","Public data","Intellectual property only","Trade secrets only"],a:0,e:["Correct. Regulated data (PHI, PII, cardholder data) has legally mandated protections.","Public data isn't restricted.","IP is protected by other means, such as patents and copyright.","Trade secrets are protected by confidentiality."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A soft-drink company's secret formula, protected only by keeping it confidential, is an example of:",c:["Trade secret","Public data","Regulated PHI","Legal data"],a:0,e:["Correct. A trade secret is valuable because it's secret, not because it's patented.","It is not public.","It isn't health information.","Legal data relates to legal matters or records."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"GDPR requires that personal data about EU residents be processed according to EU law even if stored abroad. Which concept does this relate to?",c:["Data sovereignty / jurisdiction","Data masking","Data in use","Tokenization"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country or region it relates to or where it's stored.","Masking is a protection technique.","'In use' is a data state.","Tokenization is a protection technique."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company restricts customer databases so they can only be stored in data centers within the EU. What is this?",c:["Geographic restriction / geolocation-based data control","Tokenization","Hashing","Obfuscation"],a:0,e:["Correct. Geographic restrictions keep data in approved locations, often to satisfy sovereignty laws.","Tokenization is a different technique.","Hashing is a different technique.","Obfuscation is a different technique."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which method BEST protects the confidentiality of data in transit between a browser and a web server?",c:["TLS","Full-disk encryption","Masking","Tokenization at rest"],a:0,e:["Correct. TLS encrypts data moving across the network.","FDE protects data at rest.","Masking is for display.","Tokenization protects stored values."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which statement BEST differentiates data classification 'confidential' from 'restricted' (common corporate scheme)?",c:["Restricted is typically the MOST sensitive and has the tightest access; confidential is sensitive but broader","They are the same","Confidential is public","Restricted means publicly available"],a:0,e:["Correct. In many schemes restricted is the top tier (e.g., M&A data), with confidential just below it.","Most schemes distinguish them.","Confidential isn't public.","Restricted is the opposite of public."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"An analyst needs production-like data to test a new app but must not see real customer details. Which technique keeps the data realistic yet safe?",c:["Data masking / obfuscation of the test copy","Using the production database directly","Encrypting and handing over the key","Deleting all data"],a:0,e:["Correct. Masked or anonymized data keeps its structure for testing without exposing real values.","Using production data exposes it.","Handing over the key exposes the real values.","Testing needs data."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which BEST protects data by making it unreadable to anyone without the key, while still allowing authorized decryption?",c:["Encryption","Hashing","Masking","Deletion"],a:0,e:["Correct. Encryption is reversible confidentiality.","Hashing isn't reversible.","Masking isn't key-based.","Deletion removes access for everyone."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company applies permissions so only the finance group can read the budget folder. Which data protection method is this?",c:["Permission restrictions (access control)","Tokenization","Geographic restriction","Hashing"],a:0,e:["Correct. ACL and permission restrictions control who can access the data.","Tokenization is a different technique.","Geographic restriction is a different technique.","Hashing is a different technique."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which BEST describes 'segmentation' as a data protection method?",c:["Storing sensitive data in separate network zones or systems so a breach elsewhere doesn't expose it","Encrypting every file","Masking display fields","Sending data via TLS"],a:0,e:["Correct. Isolating sensitive data limits who and what can reach it.","That's encryption.","That's masking.","That's transport protection."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which data type is BEST described as 'data that, if lost, would stop core business operations'?",c:["Critical","Public","Human-readable","Legal"],a:0,e:["Correct. Critical data is essential to operations.","Public data carries minimal impact.","'Human-readable' describes a format.","Legal data is a category by type."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which pair correctly matches data types?",c:["Human-readable: a PDF report; Non-human-readable: a compiled binary or barcode","Human-readable: a JPEG's binary data; Non-human-readable: a text file","Both are the same","Non-human-readable data is never sensitive"],a:0,e:["Correct. Human-readable data can be read directly; non-human-readable data (binary, encoded) needs software to interpret.","This pairing is backwards.","They differ.","Binary data can be very sensitive."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company digitally watermarks confidential spreadsheets and encrypts them. What does the watermark ADD?",c:["Traceability/integrity: it helps identify the source of a leak or tampering","Faster performance","Availability","Anonymity"],a:0,e:["Correct. Watermarks help trace leaks and deter misuse, while encryption provides confidentiality.","Watermarks don't speed anything up.","They don't add availability.","They do the opposite of anonymity."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A startup processes EU customers' data on US servers. Which concept MOST directly creates legal obligations regarding where and how the data is handled?",c:["Data sovereignty","Data masking","Data in use","Obfuscation"],a:0,e:["Correct. Sovereignty and jurisdiction laws such as GDPR follow the data subject's rights.","Masking is a technique.","'In use' is a data state.","Obfuscation is a technique."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of data classification?",c:["To apply appropriate protection based on sensitivity and value","To delete old data","To speed up backups","To assign IP addresses"],a:0,e:["Correct. Classification drives handling, labeling, access, encryption and retention requirements.","Deletion is retention and disposal.","Backup speed isn't the purpose.","IP addressing is unrelated."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which option is an example of obfuscation?",c:["Replacing names with pseudonyms in an analytics dataset","Encrypting with AES and sharing the key","Publishing raw data","Hashing a file to verify its download"],a:0,e:["Correct. Obfuscation (including pseudonymization) makes data hard to link to real individuals while keeping it usable.","Sharing the key exposes the data.","Publishing raw data is exposure.","That's an integrity check."]},

// ---------- 3.4 Resilience and recovery (24) ----------
{o:"3.4",q:"A company needs daily backups with minimal network and storage impact and accepts slower restores. Which backup type fits BEST?",c:["Incremental","Full every day","Differential","Mirror"],a:0,e:["Correct. Incrementals copy only the changes since the last backup of any type. They're the fastest and smallest, but a restore needs the full backup plus every incremental.","Daily fulls are the heaviest option.","Differentials grow each day until the next full.","Mirroring is continuous replication."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company does a full backup Sunday and differentials Monday through Friday. The server fails Thursday. Which backups are needed to restore?",c:["Sunday full + Wednesday differential","Sunday full + Mon, Tue, Wed differentials","Only Wednesday differential","All backups from the past month"],a:0,e:["Correct. Each differential holds all changes since the last full, so you need the full plus the latest differential.","Restoring every day's backup is how incrementals work, not differentials.","A differential alone isn't complete.","That's unnecessary."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A development team needs to roll back a VM to exactly how it was before a failed update an hour ago. What is the FASTEST option?",c:["Snapshot","Tape backup","Offsite cold storage","Differential backup"],a:0,e:["Correct. Snapshots capture point-in-time state for quick rollback.","Tape is slow to restore.","Offsite cold storage is slow to retrieve.","A differential restore is slower than reverting a snapshot."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Developers need to revert a build to any point in the past several hours, not just last night. Which approach supports this BEST?",c:["Continuous data protection / continuous backup","Weekly full backups","Monthly tapes","Differential backups"],a:0,e:["Correct. Continuous data protection records every change, so you can restore to almost any point in time.","Weekly fulls only give weekly restore points.","Monthly tapes give monthly restore points.","Differentials only give nightly restore points."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"An organization keeps a second data center with live, synchronized systems and can switch over within minutes. What type of site is this?",c:["Hot site","Warm site","Cold site","Mobile site"],a:0,e:["Correct. A hot site is fully equipped and current, so failover is fast, but it's expensive.","A warm site has equipment but needs data loaded, which takes hours to days.","A cold site is space and power only, which takes days to weeks.","A mobile site is a portable facility."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A backup facility has power, HVAC and network hookups but no servers. What type of site is this?",c:["Cold site","Warm site","Hot site","Geographic dispersion"],a:0,e:["Correct. A cold site is space and utilities only. It's the cheapest option and the slowest recovery.","A warm site has hardware.","A hot site is fully running.","Geographic dispersion is a strategy, not a site type."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company places its backup data center 500 miles away, on a different power grid and outside the same hurricane zone. What principle is this?",c:["Geographic dispersion","Clustering","Load balancing","Platform diversity"],a:0,e:["Correct. Geographic dispersion keeps a regional disaster from hitting both sites.","Clustering happens within a site.","Load balancing distributes traffic.","Platform diversity means using different technologies or vendors."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A database is copied in near real time to a second server in another city. What is this?",c:["Replication","Journaling","Snapshots","Tape rotation"],a:0,e:["Correct. Replication continuously copies data to another system or location.","Journaling logs changes locally for replay.","Snapshots are point-in-time captures.","Tape rotation is a backup schedule."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A file system records each change in a log before applying it, so after a crash it can replay or roll back to a consistent state. What is this?",c:["Journaling","Replication","Mirroring","Snapshots"],a:0,e:["Correct. Journaling tracks changes for recovery and consistency.","Replication copies data elsewhere.","Mirroring duplicates disks.","Snapshots capture a point in time."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which power component provides immediate short-term battery power so servers can shut down gracefully or ride out brief outages?",c:["UPS","Generator","PDU","Surge protector only"],a:0,e:["Correct. A UPS gives instant battery power for minutes.","A generator provides long-term power but takes time to start.","A PDU distributes power in a rack.","A surge protector handles spikes, not outages."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A data center needs power for DAYS during a regional outage. What is required in addition to a UPS?",c:["Generator","Second PDU","Surge protector","More batteries only"],a:0,e:["Correct. Generators supply extended power, and the UPS bridges the gap while they start.","A PDU doesn't generate power.","A surge protector doesn't provide power.","Batteries alone don't last days."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Three servers work together as a single system; if one fails, the others take over its workload. What is this?",c:["Clustering (failover)","Load balancing only","Journaling","Tape backup"],a:0,e:["Correct. A cluster presents several nodes as one service with failover.","Load balancing spreads load. Clusters coordinate as one system (though they often load-balance too).","Journaling is about logging changes.","Tape is backup."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company uses firewalls from two different vendors in its layered design so a single vendor vulnerability doesn't compromise both. What principle is this?",c:["Platform/vendor diversity","Geographic dispersion","Clustering","Capacity planning"],a:0,e:["Correct. Diversity avoids common-mode failures and single-vendor flaws.","Geographic dispersion is about location.","Clustering is about failover.","Capacity planning is about growth."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Recovery staff sit around a conference table and walk through their response to a simulated flood, discussing roles without touching systems. What exercise is this?",c:["Tabletop exercise","Simulation","Failover test","Parallel processing"],a:0,e:["Correct. A tabletop is discussion-based and low cost.","A simulation involves hands-on scenarios or teams.","A failover test actually switches systems.","Parallel processing is a recovery test that runs systems side by side."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"During a test, the recovery site processes live transactions ALONGSIDE the primary site to confirm results match, without shutting down the primary. What is this?",c:["Parallel processing test","Tabletop","Full interruption test","Simulation"],a:0,e:["Correct. Parallel processing runs both sites at once and compares outputs, which confirms readiness without an outage.","A tabletop is discussion only.","A full interruption test actually shuts down the primary.","A simulation practices a scenario but doesn't run production in parallel."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A red team attacks while the blue team defends in a controlled exercise. What type of test is this?",c:["Simulation","Tabletop","Parallel processing","Journaling"],a:0,e:["Correct. Simulations are hands-on scenarios, often adversarial.","A tabletop is discussion only.","Parallel processing is a DR test.","Journaling isn't a test."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"The team forecasts that user growth will exceed server and staff capacity next year and plans purchases and hiring. What is this?",c:["Capacity planning","Tabletop","Failover","Journaling"],a:0,e:["Correct. Capacity planning covers people, technology and infrastructure to meet future demand.","A tabletop is an exercise.","Failover switches to redundant systems.","Journaling is a logging mechanism."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which backup practice BEST protects against ransomware that encrypts reachable backups?",c:["Offline/immutable or air-gapped backup copies","Storing backups on the same server","Daily differentials to a mapped drive","No backups"],a:0,e:["Correct. Offline or immutable copies can't be encrypted by malware on the network. This is the core of the 3-2-1 rule.","Backups on the same server get encrypted too.","Ransomware reaches mapped drives.","No backups means no recovery."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"What should a company do regularly to ensure backups are actually usable?",c:["Test restores","Delete old backups","Encrypt them twice","Increase backup frequency only"],a:0,e:["Correct. A backup you've never restored is unproven. Test restores verify integrity and procedures.","Retention depends on policy.","Double encryption doesn't prove usability.","Frequency doesn't prove backups work."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Why should backups be encrypted?",c:["To protect the confidentiality of backup data if media is lost or stolen","To make restores faster","To remove the need for offsite storage","Because encryption prevents ransomware"],a:0,e:["Correct. Backups contain everything valuable, so lost tapes or disks must be unreadable.","Encryption usually slows restores slightly.","Offsite copies are still needed.","Ransomware can still encrypt reachable files; immutability addresses that."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which describes an 'onsite vs offsite' backup trade-off?",c:["Onsite restores faster; offsite survives site-wide disasters","Offsite is always faster","Onsite survives fire better","There's no difference"],a:0,e:["Correct. Keep both: local copies for speed and remote copies for disasters.","Offsite is usually slower to restore.","A fire destroys onsite copies.","They differ in speed and survivability."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which recovery test actually shuts down the primary site and moves all operations to the recovery site?",c:["Full interruption / failover test","Tabletop","Walkthrough","Parallel processing"],a:0,e:["Correct. It's the most realistic test and the most disruptive.","A tabletop is discussion only.","A walkthrough reviews the plan.","Parallel processing keeps the primary running."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which choice BEST describes 'high availability' vs 'fault tolerance'?",c:["HA minimizes downtime with fast failover; fault tolerance continues with zero interruption","They are identical","Fault tolerance allows long outages","HA requires no redundancy"],a:0,e:["Correct. HA recovers quickly, perhaps with a brief blip. Fault tolerance absorbs the failure with no interruption, which usually means more cost.","They're related but different.","Fault tolerance means no outage.","HA depends on redundancy."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A PDU in a rack that can be managed remotely and monitors power draw per outlet primarily helps with:",c:["Power distribution and monitoring to prevent overloads and allow remote power cycling","Generating backup power","Encrypting traffic","Replicating data"],a:0,e:["Correct. Managed PDUs distribute and monitor power. They don't generate it.","That's a generator.","That's unrelated to a PDU.","That's unrelated to a PDU."]}
]);
